Zero OS0.7 integration fixtures (private disposable images)

Files
multi-mbr.img:371195904 bytes,724992 sectors,354MiB.
  MBR partition1: start2048, count65536,32MiB ext4.
  MBR partition2: start67584, count131072,64MiB NTFS.
  MBR partition3: start198656, count524288,256MiB Btrfs.
  A1MiB gap precedes the first partition;1MiB remains after the last.
readonly-gpt.img:37748736 bytes,73728 sectors,36MiB.
  GPT partition1: start2048,last67583,count65536,32MiB ext4.
  Attribute bit60 marks this partition read-only. Both GPT headers and entry
  arrays are valid and agree. Disk GUID and partition GUID are fixed test IDs.
compatible-demo.img: an untouched copy of multi-mbr.img for user
  delivery/conversion after integrated VM validation. It contains only the
  three writable-compatible filesystem partitions, not the readonly test.
compatible-demo.vdi: dynamic VDI container,15728640 bytes,354MiB guest capacity.
  VBoxManage converted the clean raw image after the parent completed its
  initial integrated VM/offline checks. showmediuminfo accepts the container;
  conversion back to RAW is byte-identical to compatible-demo.img. This is
  container validation, not a claim of native VirtualBox guest execution.

Expected VM inventory with the original ZeroFS disk on primary master,
multi-mbr on primary slave, boot ISO on secondary master, and readonly-gpt
on secondary slave:
  A:| ZeroFS; B:| ext4; C:| NTFS; D:| Btrfs; E:| ext4 read-only.
The optical drive is not exposed as an ATA data volume.

Data and format profiles
Both ext4 images use4096-byte blocks,256-byte inodes,one block group and only
filetype+extent features. No journal,metadata checksums or64-bit descriptors.
B:|welcome contains: Zero OS ext4 volume. Files live on disk.
E:|welcome contains: This GPT volume is read-only.
The NTFS image is3.1,512-byte sectors/4096-byte clusters. Its welcome file was
added by the external fixture writer ntfscp, which expands initialized MFT
through record64, leaving free slots27..63 for Zero's bounded allocator.
C:|welcome contains: Zero OS NTFS volume. Files live on disk.
The Btrfs image is empty, single device, SINGLE metadata/data, no free-space
 tree. Exact command: mkfs.btrfs -f -m single -d single -R ^free-space-tree.
These deliberately compatible profiles exercise the documented bounded
backends; they do not establish general-purpose filesystem compatibility.

Reproduction
  bash verification/fs-0.7.0/fixtures/build-fixtures.sh
  bash verification/fs-0.7.0/fixtures/verify-zero.sh
  bash verification/fs-0.7.0/fixtures/convert-demo.sh
The build script recreates only this directory's named disposable fixtures.
It never opens build/data.img,build/data.vdi or a physical device for writing.
External e2fsprogs,ntfs-3g andbtrfs-progs only generate/validate test filesystem
contents. Their paths can be overridden with ZERO_EXT4_E2FSPROGS,
ZERO_NTFS_TOOLS,ZERO_BTRFS_TOOLS. They are not part of the guest implementation.
partition-tables.zero is the original Zero metadata author for MBR and both
GPT copies, including IEEE CRC32. No external partition creator is invoked.
Host truncate/dd/cmp/cp perform image assembly and byte comparisons only.

Verification
Independent partx confirms partition starts/lengths. e2fsck -fn reports clean
ext4 images. ntfscat verifies NTFS welcome and ntfsinfo records the expanded
MFT; ntfsfix -n is a limited NTFS consistency check, not Windows chkdsk.
btrfs check --readonly reports no errors. Extracted partition contents compare
byte-for-byte with their checked source filesystem images.
The exact Zero discovery FSM, under a hosted read-only adapter, discovers A-E
with correct physical indices, offsets and E's retained read-only bit. The
exact three filesystem FSMs accept their nonzero partition offsets and read
ext4/NTFS welcome or probe the empty Btrfs volume successfully.
IMAGES.sha256 records original fixture bytes; READONLY.sha256 is the immutable
GPT baseline to compare after attempted guest mutations. Use private copies
for writable VM tests, so these baselines remain available.
VDI.sha256 records the delivered VDI bytes. convert-demo.sh refuses existing
VDI/roundtrip targets; it uses an isolated VirtualBox configuration directory.
Attach the demo VDI as primary IDE slave (port0,device1), keeping the existing
ZeroFS data disk as primary master. Then its partitions appear as B:|,C:|,D:|.
